This thing scans over the wound, measures the electric field,

454
00:32:10,961 --> 00:32:16,020
and then we would have another device that would put electric,

455
00:32:16,200 --> 00:32:19,600
enhance that field to stimulate wound healing of that wound.

456
00:32:19,800 --> 00:32:25,880
We have demonstrated in animals again that you can enhance the rate of wound healing with an electric field.

457
00:32:26,580 --> 00:32:28,600
Again, nothing inhuman yet.

458
00:32:29,120 --> 00:32:29,820
Dr.

459
00:32:29,821 --> 00:32:33,700
Munir LaRussi has been experimenting with an instrument that uses plasma to heal wounds.

460
00:32:33,701 --> 00:32:42,860
The plasma is a force state of matter, so it's been around since the world was created.

461
00:32:42,861 --> 00:32:51,400
This is a device that is able to emit a long plume, we call that a plume of plasma.

462
00:32:51,401 --> 00:32:54,800
It's kind of a beam that shoots out of ionized gas.

463
00:32:54,801 --> 00:32:56,238
Now this device, Dr.

464
00:32:56,239 --> 00:33:00,840
McCoy would have loved to have it on the Enterprise because this

465
00:33:00,841 --> 00:33:08,320
This plume is able to actually inactivate or kill bacteria that can infect wounds or burns.

466
00:33:08,321 --> 00:33:11,400
If you don't get him to sickbay, he will die.

467
00:33:12,100 --> 00:33:23,040
A lot of things that people have seen in Star Trek have inspired engineers, scientists, doctors to try to create.

468
00:33:24,200 --> 00:33:28,840
And so that's another great thing about Star Trek, is that it inspires people to do things.

469
00:33:28,841 --> 00:33:34,980
One of the first designs of a humanoid robot was made by Leonardo da Vinci around 1495.

470
00:33:36,000 --> 00:33:41,200
I'm entering the ship's holodeck where images of reality can be created by our computer.

471
00:33:41,780 --> 00:33:43,160
Highly useful in crew training.

472
00:33:44,160 --> 00:33:47,000
Highly enjoyable when used for games and recreation.

473
00:33:47,400 --> 00:33:50,460
The holodeck is actually one of the more realistic aspects of Star Trek.

474
00:33:50,461 --> 00:33:54,600
It's really the natural continuation of something that's going on right now, virtual reality.

475
00:33:54,601 --> 00:34:01,960
Virtual reality technology was becoming something of an issue and gaining some prominence in the 1980s.

476
00:34:02,100 --> 00:34:06,120
And Gene thought, hey, what would the ultimate virtual reality experience be like?

477
00:34:06,260 --> 00:34:08,020
Well, totally immersive.

478
00:34:08,480 --> 00:34:12,500
The holodeck is short for holographic environment simulator.

479
00:34:12,880 --> 00:34:18,380
It's a room in which you can recreate any environment, any setting, any person.

480
00:34:18,381 --> 00:34:23,260
They were trying to figure out a way of making the Enterprise D homier,

481
00:34:23,600 --> 00:34:26,120
because that is where you live.

482
00:34:26,280 --> 00:34:29,880
One of the thoughts is that you'd have a big wall-sized television.

483
00:34:30,300 --> 00:34:35,900
I think that eventually led to the concept that maybe it could become an immersive environment.

484
00:34:36,120 --> 00:34:42,800
For us as writers, it was a way that we could do a period piece Sherlock Holmes story,

485
00:34:42,801 --> 00:34:47,380
or some sort of fantasy planet or place that we hadn't created,

486
00:34:47,381 --> 00:34:52,380
or go back to somebody's home when they were in the middle of space.

487
00:34:53,180 --> 00:34:55,180
Program desired location.

488
00:34:55,540 --> 00:34:58,720
Earth, United States, San Francisco, California.

489
00:34:59,180 --> 00:34:59,920
Time period.

490
00:35:00,700 --> 00:35:03,040
1941 A.D.

491
00:35:03,440 --> 00:35:08,680
Imagine the kinds of video games that people play today, times a thousand,

492
00:35:08,681 --> 00:35:12,800
where you're truly immersed in an artificial environment of your own creation,

493
00:35:13,180 --> 00:35:16,700
and it, for all intents and purposes, feels absolutely real.

494
00:35:17,380 --> 00:35:21,840
The characters our meet are generated by the computer, of course, yet they feel real.

495
00:35:22,480 --> 00:35:24,200
They seem real in every way.

496
00:35:24,620 --> 00:35:27,480
One of the first times we ever saw the holodeck in Star Trek The Next Generation

497
00:35:27,481 --> 00:35:30,180
was an episode where they used it for Aikido training.

498
00:35:30,420 --> 00:35:33,560
So all you had is these figures and Aikido geese.

499
00:35:33,660 --> 00:35:34,780
So that was very simplistic.

500
00:35:35,360 --> 00:35:38,700
One of the next times we saw the holodeck was an episode called The Big Goodbye

501
00:35:38,701 --> 00:35:43,940
where they recreated the world of gumshoe detective Dixon Hill.

502
00:35:43,941 --> 00:35:45,580
I am not Dixon Hill.

503
00:35:45,581 --> 00:35:48,580
I just look like Dixon Hill

504
00:35:48,581 --> 00:35:50,160
It was a great storytelling device

505
00:35:50,161 --> 00:35:52,960
and of course there's always the classic holodeck malfunction

506
00:35:52,961 --> 00:35:55,060
I can't find the captain sir

507
00:35:55,061 --> 00:35:56,900
I told you he's in the holodeck

508
00:35:56,901 --> 00:35:58,400
I'm at the holodeck

509
00:35:58,401 --> 00:35:59,920
something's gone wrong

510
00:35:59,921 --> 00:36:01,700
It actually was a visionary

511
00:36:01,701 --> 00:36:05,000
thing that Gene Roddenberry came up with

512
00:36:05,001 --> 00:36:07,300
That's when we're able to open our eyes

513
00:36:07,301 --> 00:36:09,740
and see things as they really could be

514
00:36:15,580 --> 00:36:17,400
You'll have to call again. I'm just leaving.

515
00:36:18,740 --> 00:36:20,980
I'm not dressed properly. I'll be back.

516
00:36:21,560 --> 00:36:27,940
Holodeck always worried me, though, because if you found an environment that you really liked, why would you leave?

517
00:36:28,220 --> 00:36:29,820
What a great playground, right?

518
00:36:29,980 --> 00:36:32,860
You would absolutely never move from one spot.

519
00:36:36,680 --> 00:36:41,540
To explain this immersive 3D environment further, we must first enter the holodeck.

520
00:36:41,541 --> 00:36:46,860
Once inside, the holodeck appears as just one large room with four walls, a ceiling, and a floor.

521
00:36:47,160 --> 00:36:51,180
But it's the computerized grid that covers the entire room that you notice first.

522
00:36:51,520 --> 00:36:56,340
The grid acts as a skin, and it's within this skin that makes up the building blocks of the holodeck.

523
00:36:56,740 --> 00:37:01,460
Millions of tiny omnidirectional holo-diodes create the effects of the virtual environment.

524
00:37:01,461 --> 00:37:07,080
Each diode can project full-color stereoscopic images that make up the real-life look of the holodeck.

525
00:37:07,340 --> 00:37:11,160
But it's the manipulated force fields that allow each image to come to life.

526
00:37:11,540 --> 00:37:14,920
The force fields allow a visitor to feel objects that aren't really there.

527
00:37:15,340 --> 00:37:19,740
A Starfleet officer can program the holodeck to display any environment he or she wants,

528
00:37:20,120 --> 00:37:22,880
as long as the computer already has the simulation and memory.

529
00:37:29,700 --> 00:37:31,840
Captain, you need the diversion.

530
00:37:35,940 --> 00:37:37,060
Dixon Hill.

531
00:37:38,880 --> 00:37:40,620
The program's installed and waiting.

532
00:37:40,621 --> 00:37:50,233
So in the holodeck we have various kinds of components, these omnidirectional holographic diodes, the holographic arrays, the projector arrays, the force field

533
00:37:50,234 --> 00:37:53,800
generators, the replicators, the matter energy replicators.

534
00:37:53,801 --> 00:38:02,680
All of that technology in some way that for us seems like magic goes into the creation of this extraordinary technology called the holodeck.

535
00:38:02,681 --> 00:38:09,240
The holodeck is perhaps the greatest entertainment apparatus invented

536
00:38:09,241 --> 00:38:14,380
as it allows the person who enters it to be transformed to another time and place in history

537
00:38:14,381 --> 00:38:16,640
and to interact with real-life people.

538
00:38:17,560 --> 00:38:19,960
Inside the holodeck, one could have lunch with a president,

539
00:38:20,540 --> 00:38:24,180
take a maiden voyage on a sailing ship, or witness a piece of history.

540
00:38:24,181 --> 00:38:27,300
It's based on the idea of holograms, which are certainly realistic.

541
00:38:27,720 --> 00:38:29,180
They're three-dimensional images.

542
00:38:29,181 --> 00:38:36,800
Where we are with holograms today is that holography is, for still images, is very, very well developed.

543
00:38:37,180 --> 00:38:40,660
It's like high-quality photography, and it gives this additional richness.

544
00:38:41,640 --> 00:38:47,180
Holographic television, on the other hand, is about where ordinary television was in the 1930s.

545
00:38:48,860 --> 00:38:54,120
At the forefront of immersive virtual reality is Professor Rachel Brady at Duke University.

546
00:38:54,121 --> 00:39:00,500
What I'm interested in is how can we interact and look at three-dimensional data in a more natural way.

547
00:39:01,240 --> 00:39:05,580
So I wrote a proposal and built the Dive, the Duke Immersal Virtual Environment,

548
00:39:05,800 --> 00:39:09,720
which allows us to interact and look at 3D data in three dimensions.

549
00:39:10,420 --> 00:39:15,600
Each wall has a projector sending the image to it, which is bouncing off this mirror

550
00:39:15,601 --> 00:39:21,820
so that we can get a big enough image to cover the whole wall without having to have such a big room.

551
00:39:21,821 --> 00:39:25,620
So this application is a way to study deep brain anatomy.

552
00:39:25,920 --> 00:39:28,300
We have an MRI of a human brain,

553
00:39:28,400 --> 00:39:33,220
and we have difficulty in looking at how the structures intercorrelate with each other.

554
00:39:33,320 --> 00:39:40,480
So we can take this MRI slice and pull it towards the 3D structure

555
00:39:40,481 --> 00:39:44,200
and see how it intersects with the MRI.

556
00:39:44,201 --> 00:39:54,200
I'm rather optimistic eventually about our being able to make very, very rich spaces

557
00:39:54,201 --> 00:39:56,900
with three-dimensional objects moving around in them.

558
00:39:57,240 --> 00:40:01,220
We're a little bit away from actually implementing the holodeck because we have no sense of touch.

559
00:40:01,400 --> 00:40:02,900
Maybe we should be getting back to the Enterprise.

560
00:40:03,620 --> 00:40:04,700
We are on the Enterprise.

561
00:40:05,500 --> 00:40:07,180
Oh, yes. Of course, sir, we are.

562
00:40:07,540 --> 00:40:10,460
You could imagine anything and have these fantasy adventures

563
00:40:10,461 --> 00:40:12,880
and quite a number of the different Star Treks

564
00:40:12,881 --> 00:40:15,840
use the holodeck to put us into different periods.

565
00:40:15,980 --> 00:40:17,780
Gene thought, hey, you know, if we're going to do the holodeck,

566
00:40:18,060 --> 00:40:19,060
let's do it big time.

567
00:40:19,160 --> 00:40:21,920
Let's really make this the ultimate immersive reality.

568
00:40:22,540 --> 00:40:24,420
There were, from the point of view of the user,

569
00:40:24,520 --> 00:40:25,820
there seemed to be no limits.

570
00:40:25,980 --> 00:40:28,220
Even though you're surrounded by four walls,

571
00:40:28,700 --> 00:40:31,820
you can never get to them because the landscape keeps receding

572
00:40:31,821 --> 00:40:33,340
and that little treadmill under the feet

573
00:40:33,341 --> 00:40:36,680
makes sure that you're not really moving relative to the actual walls.

574
00:40:37,120 --> 00:40:38,320
It just seems that way.

575
00:40:38,321 --> 00:40:44,120
Right now, we don't know how to put light in the air such that you bump into it and you feel it.

576
00:40:44,560 --> 00:40:49,380
And we don't know how to make light go out halfway across the room and make a U-turn and come back

577
00:40:49,381 --> 00:40:53,260
without having something there for it to reflect from.

578
00:40:53,261 --> 00:40:57,760
The point is, not so much could these things be realized,

579
00:40:58,060 --> 00:41:02,860
but that the imagination itself can play and think ahead.

580
00:41:02,980 --> 00:41:06,060
And that will produce new things, new ideas in the real world.

581
00:41:06,061 --> 00:41:12,980
There are some physics principles that are to some degree at odds with what you'd like the holodeck to do.

582
00:41:14,020 --> 00:41:15,860
Memory, save current setting.

583
00:41:16,480 --> 00:41:17,720
Current setting saved.

584
00:41:18,280 --> 00:41:18,980
Holodeck off.

585
00:41:20,020 --> 00:41:28,560
Immersive virtual reality environments are certainly, you know, part of amusement parks as well as research parks.

586
00:41:28,820 --> 00:41:31,000
The science behind the holodeck is pretty shaky.

587
00:41:31,001 --> 00:41:35,800
It doesn't really, you know, it's not really there, you know, bending proto-matter and things like that.

588
00:41:35,801 --> 00:41:42,700
Those are words that were made up for the series, but just as in teleportation and as a warp drive, who knows what we'll end up with.

589
00:41:42,940 --> 00:41:52,960
Holography right now is like a window, which is to say that maybe Picard could look at this window and see some virtual scene behind it,

590
00:41:53,040 --> 00:41:56,620
but he can't step through the window and be in the room with those objects.

591
00:41:57,100 --> 00:42:00,120
And in order to do the holodeck, we have to solve that problem also.

592
00:42:02,560 --> 00:42:03,260
Exit.

593
00:42:05,800 --> 00:42:11,660
The holodeck world of Star Trek in the sense of having a virtual reality that you live in is not that far off.

594
00:42:12,040 --> 00:42:14,260
It's certainly not as far off as the 24th century.

595
00:42:14,261 --> 00:42:22,340
There are many things that Star Trek just stumbled upon and created that now really do exist.

596
00:42:22,500 --> 00:42:27,260
Science fiction is not so much fiction as it truly is.

597
00:42:28,380 --> 00:42:31,260
Our imagination meeting our love of science.

598
00:42:31,261 --> 00:42:36,540
Science fiction, you know, becomes reality when reality catches up with science fiction.

599
00:42:36,880 --> 00:42:43,260
It is amazing to me that many of the things that Star Trek has invented, they either exist or they're very close to existing.

600
00:42:43,460 --> 00:42:48,753
We will never know it all. We will never arrive. There is no place to arrive.

601
00:42:48,754 --> 00:42:52,740
It is truly about the journey. And the journey is eternal.

602
00:42:52,741 --> 00:42:59,740
And the fact that Star Trek took that huge leap of faith to try to push in that direction,

603
00:42:59,741 --> 00:43:02,740
it's stimulating in and of itself.

604
00:43:02,741 --> 00:43:05,740
They got a lot right, they got some stuff wrong, but it was okay.

605
00:43:05,741 --> 00:43:09,740
It made for a really good series and movies.

606
00:43:09,741 --> 00:43:13,740
Star Trek offers us a vision of a future full of possibilities.

607
00:43:13,741 --> 00:43:17,740
Whether these possibilities are realized or not remains to be seen.

608
00:43:17,741 --> 00:43:21,740
Some are outlandish, some might be right around the corner.

609
00:43:21,741 --> 00:43:26,420
But one thing is certain, science is continuing to progress at an incredible rate.

610
00:43:26,720 --> 00:43:31,680
And nature continues to surprise us in ways that no science fiction writer has ever dreamed of.

611
00:43:31,681 --> 00:43:35,160
So it is true now, and it will be true in the 23rd century.

612
00:43:36,000 --> 00:43:38,940
Truth will remain stranger than fiction.

